Task 1: Install Grafana
Subtask 1.1: Update System Packages
First, ensure your system packages are up to date:

sudo apt update
sudo apt upgrade -y
Subtask 1.2: Install Required Dependencies
Install necessary packages for Grafana installation:

sudo apt install -y software-properties-common apt-transport-https wget curl
Subtask 1.3: Add Grafana Repository
Add the official Grafana GPG key and repository:

# Add GPG key
wget -q -O - https://packages.grafana.com/gpg.key | sudo apt-key add -

# Add repository
echo "deb https://packages.grafana.com/oss/deb stable main" | sudo tee -a /etc/apt/sources.list.d/grafana.list
Subtask 1.4: Install Grafana
Update package list and install Grafana:

sudo apt update
sudo apt install -y grafana
Subtask 1.5: Verify Installation
Check if Grafana has been installed successfully:

grafana-server --version
You should see output showing the Grafana version number.

Task 2: Set up Grafana Server
Subtask 2.1: Enable Grafana Service
Enable Grafana to start automatically on system boot:

sudo systemctl enable grafana-server
Subtask 2.2: Start Grafana Service
Start the Grafana server service:

sudo systemctl start grafana-server
Subtask 2.3: Check Service Status
Verify that Grafana is running properly:

sudo systemctl status grafana-server
You should see active (running) in the output, indicating the service is working correctly.

Subtask 2.4: Verify Network Connectivity
Check if Grafana is listening on the default port 3000:

sudo netstat -tlnp | grep 3000
You should see Grafana listening on port 3000.

Subtask 2.5: Test Local Connection
Test the connection to Grafana locally:

curl -I http://localhost:3000
You should receive an HTTP response indicating the server is accessible.

